Found while closing karr #84, not caused by it, and NOT caught by its guard --
that guard measures our own emitter and parser, and both agree with each other
here. The disagreement is with Go.

YAML::XS (libyaml, YAML 1.1 resolution as it implements it) reads a leading-zero
integer as DECIMAL. Go`s yaml.v3 reads it as OCTAL. The digest covers our
number, the document carries the source spelling, and Go re-derives a different
number from it. Measured, sops 3.13.3, leaf under _unencrypted, one document
per row:

  source  our int  document  sops -d
  010     10       010       exit 51 MAC mismatch   (Go: 8)
  017     17       017       exit 51 MAC mismatch   (Go: 15)
  0755    755      0755      exit 51 MAC mismatch   (Go: 493)
  007     7        007       exit 0                 (7 either way -- agrees by luck)
  0o10    "0o10"   0o10      exit 51 MAC mismatch   (we type it str, Go reads int 8)

`mode: 0755` in a config file is the realistic form of this, and the file is
written silently. JSON is unaffected in itself (JSON has no octal), and since
karr #84 an 010 on its way into JSON is refused rather than written.

Note the last row is the mirror image: for `0o10` OUR parser says str and Go
says int, so a guard that only looked at int leaves would miss it.

Not fixable by pattern-matching the text (ADR 0002) and not visible to an
emit-and-reparse through our own parser, which is why karr #84`s guard cannot
see it. The honest candidates are (a) refuse a leaf whose SOURCE spelling this
library cannot prove Go resolves the same way, which needs a rule about text
after all, or (b) quote such a leaf on the way out, which changes its type. Both
move wire bytes and want an ADR and a maintainer decision.

Wire lane. Measured with sops 3.13.3, Crypt::Age 0.002.
Closed by 706ac4a (ADR 0013, t/30-foreign-resolution-guard.t).

Candidate (a), as decided: a leaf whose SOURCE SPELLING this library cannot
prove Go resolves the way it does is refused at encrypt time. Quoting on the
way out was not taken -- it changes the leafs type, which sops never does.

The rule is not "a leading zero". It is a model of yaml.v3s resolve() plus
sopss ToBytes, applied to the token the emitter would actually write, and
verified branch by branch against the binary:

  007 passes   -- 7 is 7 in both bases, digest bytes equal, sops -d exit 0
  0o10 caught  -- WE type it str and digest "0o10", Go reads the integer 8
  0755 caught  -- 755 here, 493 there

and with them 010, 017, -010, +010, 0O10, 0o7, 0o0, 0x1f, 0X1F, -0x1f, +0o10,
0b101, 0b0, 1_000, 0_7, 0_, 685_230.15, 1_0.5, .inf/.Inf/.INF/+.inf/-.inf,
.nan/.NaN, Null, NULL, TRUE, FALSE, 2015-01-01, 2015-1-2, 2015-01-01 12:00:00,
2015-01-01t12:00:00Z, 2016-02-29 and a bare 0xffffffffffffffff.

Measured, sops 3.13.3, 648-row emitter corpus (162 leaves x 2 slots x both
handlers), before/after: 53 rows move, ALL 53 already failed -- 50 sops -d exit
51, 3 exit 25 (a uint64 sops refuses to write at all). The other 595 rows are
byte-identical. Nothing that worked stops working.

Also asked, since it decides the error message: sops -e on a plaintext
mode: 0755 stores type:int with the value 493 and prints 493 on -d. The
spelling does not survive sops -e at all, which is why the READ path needs
nothing here and why the message names 493 as what to pass instead.

Not guarded, each measured and each stated in the ADR: encrypted slots (an
ENC[...] string, 0 of 324 rows affected), JSON (quotes every string, has no
octal; the int leaves are already refused by #84s guard, 0 JSON rows move),
the plaintext emitters behind decrypt_file and edit (no MAC to disagree with),
the sops metadata section (not covered by the digest), and mac_only_encrypted
documents -- filed as #87, because there the digest does not cover an
unencrypted leaf and refusing would refuse a document that works today.

